                <pb facs="0206" n="122" sameAs="frus:frus1903/pg122"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr. Conger.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1903-10-09"><hi rendition="#italic">October 9,
                        1903</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 718.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch No. 1372, of August 21 last, inclosing a copy of a note received by the
                    dean of the diplomatic corps from Prince Ch’ing, advising that thenceforth
                    foreign merchants will not be permitted to buy property in Peking for dwellings
                    or to establish places of business in that city.</p>
                <p>The Department approves your course in not making any reply to the note at
                    present, but leaving the matter open for such future discussion as events may
                    require.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
